<%#
  A stack of comment cards plus a reply box, per PLAN.md screen 5 and
  DESIGN.md §8. D renders these into `threads_<block.id>` on page load; E
  appends/replaces them by `thread_<thread.node_id>` afterward.

  The card sits on the base surface in every state — what a thread's state
  changes is the 3px band down its left edge and the words in its badges, not
  a tint across the whole panel.

  **Resolve is a thread-level action and lives in the thread's foot**, beside
  the reply box, not in a strip above the first comment. The two controls in
  that row are the only two things you can do to a conversation — reply to it,
  or close it — and putting the second one where it used to be cost every
  thread a band of empty space above its first word, with a lone button
  floating at the right of it (reported 2026-09-26). It costs nothing there:
  the reply box rests at one line, and whether `viewer_can_resolve` is true or
  false only changes the row's width, never its height.

  The head row above the comments is now badges only (Outdated, On removed
  content) — information, not a control — and is still rendered only when it
  has something to carry, so an ordinary thread opens straight onto its first
  comment.

  A **resolved** thread is one row: the badge, the Show/Hide disclosure and
  Unresolve, all on the summary line. Unresolve cannot sit inside the
  `<summary>` (a click inside one toggles the disclosure as well) and cannot
  sit after it (everything after a `<summary>` is the disclosure content, so
  it would vanish when collapsed), so it is a sibling of the `<details>`,
  positioned into the summary's own row by `.thread-resolved`.

  Locals:
    thread:         (required) Github::Types::ReviewThread
    pull_request:    accepted for callers that already have one, but nothing
                     below reads it — every URL here is built from
                     params[:owner]/repo/number instead, so a caller with no
                     pull_request to hand (resolve/unresolve, which resolve
                     from the mutation's own returned thread rather than a
                     refetch) can safely pass nil.
    pending_review:  Github::Types::Review or nil
    block_id:        the block this thread is rendered under, or nil when
                     re-rendering standalone (reply/resolve/delete) — only
                     `create` needs it, to also clear the composer slot.
%>
<% thread = local_assigns.fetch(:thread) %>
<% pending_review = local_assigns[:pending_review] %>

<% if thread.present? %>
  <% modifier =
       if thread.resolved?
         "thread--resolved"
       elsif thread.outdated?
         "thread--outdated"
       elsif thread.comments.any?(&:pending?)
         "thread--pending"
       end %>

  <%# Badges only. The resolve toggle used to share this row, which is what
      made an ordinary thread open onto an empty strip. %>
  <% badges = capture do %>
    <% if thread.outdated? %>
      <span class="pill-modified">Outdated</span>
    <% end %>
    <% if thread.left_side? %>
      <span class="pill-neutral">On removed content</span>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# The thread-level toggle, rendered once and placed in one of two rows:
      the foot while the conversation is open, the summary row while it is
      resolved and collapsed. `viewer_can_*` decides whether it exists at all
      — GitHub already knows, and a repo admin can resolve a thread they have
      no comment in. %>
  <% resolve = capture do %>
    <% if thread.viewer_can_resolve %>
      <%= button_to "Resolve",
                     repo_pull_thread_resolve_path(owner: params[:owner], repo: params[:repo], number: params[:number], id: thread.node_id),
                     method: :post, class: "btn btn-ghost btn-sm",
                     form: { data: { turbo: true } }, data: { testid: "thread-resolve" } %>
    <% elsif thread.viewer_can_unresolve %>
      <%= button_to "Unresolve",
                     repo_pull_thread_unresolve_path(owner: params[:owner], repo: params[:repo], number: params[:number], id: thread.node_id),
                     method: :post, class: "btn btn-ghost btn-sm",
                     form: { data: { turbo: true } }, data: { testid: "thread-unresolve" } %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <% body = capture do %>
    <%# Its own id, separate from the reply box below it, so a new reply can
        be appended here directly (ReviewCommentsController#reply) without
        needing to rebuild the whole thread from a review_threads refetch —
        performance fix requested 2026-09-19 ("saving a comment feels slow").
        `.thread-comments` draws the hairline between one comment and the
        next: each comment renders inside its own wrapper, so the card itself
        can never see its siblings. %>
    <div id="thread_comments_<%= thread.node_id %>" class="thread-comments">
      <% thread.comments.each do |comment| %>
        <%= render "review_comments/comment", comment: comment, thread_id: thread.node_id %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <div class="thread-foot">
      <%= render "review_comments/reply_form", thread: thread, pending_review: pending_review %>
      <%# Resolve rides beside the reply box. While the thread is resolved the
          toggle has already been spent on the summary row above, so this is
          empty and the reply box simply runs the whole width. %>
      <% if !thread.resolved? && resolve.to_s.strip.present? %>
        <div class="thread-foot-actions"><%= resolve %></div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <div id="thread_<%= thread.node_id %>" class="thread <%= modifier %>" data-testid="thread"
       data-thread-id="<%= thread.node_id %>">
    <%# `capture` answers nil, not "", when the block wrote nothing at all —
        a thread with no badge at all. A resolved thread carries its badges on
        the summary row instead, so it never draws this strip. %>
    <% if !thread.resolved? && badges.to_s.strip.present? %>
      <div class="thread-head"><%= badges %></div>
    <% end %>

    <% if thread.resolved? %>
      <%# Collapsed by default; native <details> so it works with JS off, and
          collapse_controller.js only swaps the Show/Hide word. %>
      <div class="thread-resolved">
        <details class="thread-collapse" data-controller="collapse" data-testid="thread-body">
          <summary class="thread-summary">
            <span class="pill-resolved" data-testid="thread-resolved-badge">
              Resolved<% if thread.resolved_by %> by <%= thread.resolved_by.login %><% end %>
            </span>
            <%= badges %>
            <span class="thread-summary-toggle">
              <span data-collapse-target="label">Show</span> the conversation
            </span>
          </summary>
          <%= body %>
        </details>

        <% if resolve.to_s.strip.present? %>
          <div class="thread-resolved-actions"><%= resolve %></div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% else %>
      <div data-testid="thread-body"><%= body %></div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>
